  "id": "Compound::DB00180 - Gene::2908",
  "verbalization": "Flunisolide, Fluorometholone, and Beclomethasone are all glucocorticoids, a class of compounds that bind to the glucocorticoid receptor (Nr3c1), a nuclear receptor involved in the regulation of gene expression in response to corticosteroids. The evidence suggests that Flunisolide and its related compounds, such as Fluorometholone, cause hyperadrenocorticism or nasal septum perforation, which are conditions associated with excessive glucocorticoid activity. This indicates that Flunisolide may act through a common mechanism involving the glucocorticoid receptor, as all the compounds share a common ancestor in the glucocorticoid family. The binding of these compounds to Nr3c1 appears to be a key step in their biological activity, suggesting that Flunisolide may exert its effects by modulating Nr3c1 function. The evidence supports a direct relationship between Flunisolide and Nr3c1, mediated through glucocorticoid receptor activation, and highlights the role of glucocorticoids in the regulation of gene expression.",
